#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

# requires: foot cal readlink dirname

# A calendar, from clicking the clock in the bar.
#
# Three months - last, this, next - because the question that sends you to a
# calendar is almost always "what date is that Thursday" or "how far away is
# that", and both need the months either side.
#
# WHY NOT A CALENDAR APPLICATION
#
# Because there is nothing to integrate with. No mail, no events, no accounts:
# this machine has no calendar data, so a calendar application would be a large
# GTK dependency rendering exactly what `cal` already prints. The repository's
# rule is that new tooling earns its place, and this does not.
#
# The clock's tooltip also shows a calendar on hover, which is waybar's own and
# costs nothing. This is the click version, for when you want it to stay put.
#
# OPAQUE, UNLIKE EVERY OTHER TERMINAL HERE
#
# foot is configured at alpha 0.90, which is right for a window you work in and
# wrong for this one: a calendar is dense small text read for two seconds, and
# whatever is behind it shows straight through the gaps between the numbers.
# Rendered over btop it was genuinely hard to read. The override applies to this
# window only, so the setting itself is untouched.
#
# colors-dark rather than colors: the bare [colors] section is deprecated, and
# naming it made foot print a deprecation warning into the calendar - one line
# of the eleven this window has, spent telling the user off.

# Toggling rather than opening: clicking the clock twice should put the calendar
# away, not open a second one. The app_id here is what both the toggle and the
# floating window rule match on, so all three have to agree.
#
# Its sibling is called by path, not by name. This script is launched by waybar,
# which runs as a systemd user service whose PATH is /usr/bin and friends -
# ~/.local/bin is put there by .zshrc, which applies to interactive shells and
# nothing else. A bare `sway-toggle-window` here resolves when this is run from
# a terminal and fails from the bar, which is exactly how it was found.
exec "$(dirname "$(readlink -f "$0")")/sway-toggle-window" calendar \
    foot \
        --app-id=calendar \
        --title=Calendar \
        --window-size-chars=64x11 \
        --override=colors-dark.alpha=1.0 \
        -e sh -c 'cal -3 --color=always; read -rsn1'
